1
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:08,520
For today, we're going to be talking about autonomous

2
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:11,280
learning, specifically in the context of reinforcement

3
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:12,800
learning for robots

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:15,440
And this lecture and the next lecture

5
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:18,040
are going to be focusing a bit more on robotics use

6
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:21,040
cases, where today, we'll be talking about autonomy

7
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:23,640
And on Wednesday, we'll be talking about--

8
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:26,240
we have a guest lecture talking about sim to real transfer

9
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:28,840
and humanoid robots

10
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:32,560
So we'll be talking about why robots aren't autonomous

11
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:35,840
already, defining the problem of autonomous reinforcement

12
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:36,720
learning

13
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:38,640
And then developing the algorithms

14
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:40,520
specifically thinking about learning policies

15
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:43,480
without human intervention, and a formulation

16
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:46,880
of reinforcement learning called single life reinforcement

17
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:48,880
learning

18
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:51,280
So the goal here will be to think

19
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:54,800
about how we can imbue intelligent machines

20
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:58,860
with autonomy, the ability to allow them to, on their own

21
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:03,270
learn, interact, practice, get better

22
00:01:03,270 --> 00:01:06,550
without a human in the loop

23
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:07,570
Cool

24
00:01:07,570 --> 00:01:12,690
So let's start with some of the motivation

25
00:01:12,690 --> 00:01:15,130
So we've been talking a lot about reinforcement learning

26
00:01:15,130 --> 00:01:17,350
We also talked a little bit about imitation learning

27
00:01:17,350 --> 00:01:19,350
where imitation learning, typically, we

28
00:01:19,350 --> 00:01:23,550
are training an agent to mimic some demonstrations that

29
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:25,090
are provided from an expert

30
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:26,890
Whereas, in reinforcement learning

31
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:29,790
the agent is practicing through trial and error

32
00:01:29,790 --> 00:01:32,670
And this notion of practicing is something

33
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:36,390
that is already more autonomous because the agent is

34
00:01:36,390 --> 00:01:39,670
learning from its own experience in the world

35
00:01:39,670 --> 00:01:43,470
But it actually isn't entirely autonomous in practice

36
00:01:43,470 --> 00:01:45,470
when you think about running these algorithms

37
00:01:45,470 --> 00:01:48,670
on physical robots

38
00:01:48,670 --> 00:01:50,950
And in particular, if we think about saying

39
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:54,310
that we want some robot that's going from some initial state

40
00:01:54,310 --> 00:02:00,330
distribution, some distribution over initial states

41
00:02:00,330 --> 00:02:02,430
So this is the starting conditions

42
00:02:02,430 --> 00:02:04,670
that our agent might be faced with

43
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:10,389
when it's actually deployed to some region of the state

44
00:02:10,389 --> 00:02:12,390
space that has high reward

45
00:02:12,390 --> 00:02:16,210
Then, typically when we think about this learning process

46
00:02:16,210 --> 00:02:18,550
we will collect trials

47
00:02:18,550 --> 00:02:20,930
So for example, maybe at the beginning

48
00:02:20,930 --> 00:02:23,390
the first attempt at the task will look something

49
00:02:23,390 --> 00:02:29,130
like this, where because we haven't learned anything yet

50
00:02:29,130 --> 00:02:30,890
maybe we haven't practiced very much

51
00:02:30,890 --> 00:02:35,270
we don't have a trajectory that is very good

52
00:02:35,270 --> 00:02:38,050
And then from there, typically, we've thought about, OK

53
00:02:38,050 --> 00:02:40,890
can we retry the task and attempt the task again

54
00:02:40,890 --> 00:02:44,230
where we, again, start from a state in our initial state

55
00:02:44,230 --> 00:02:50,590
distribution and rollout our policy again

56
00:02:50,590 --> 00:02:53,930
Now when we're rolling out these policies

57
00:02:53,930 --> 00:03:01,340
we are sampling actions from our policy distribution

58
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:06,240
and acting according to the agent's own actions

59
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:08,780
And so each of these trajectories

60
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:13,380
are under the control of the robot or the agent

61
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:16,000
And as you're rolling out your policy

62
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,100
this part is fully autonomous

63
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:22,860
Control is fully with the policy pi

64
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:25,580
But one thing, one little catch is

65
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:28,860
that after you've attempted the task, once you're

66
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:30,380
in a state over here

67
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:32,860
And we've been thinking about when you then

68
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:35,080
attempt the task again, magically

69
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:37,460
you'll somehow get back over here

70
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:41,060
and attempt the task from this starting place

71
00:03:41,060 --> 00:03:43,220
And we haven't really talked about how do you

72
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:48,380
get from here, all the way back to here

73
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:51,780
And in practice, when you take traditional reinforcement

74
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:53,860
learning algorithms that we've been talking about

75
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:57,130
we've been assuming that a human or, somehow, you'll

76
00:03:57,130 --> 00:03:59,630
magically get reset to this initial starting place

77
00:03:59,630 --> 00:04:03,250
And in practice, that might mean that a human is actually

78
00:04:03,250 --> 00:04:05,130
physically moving the robot or physically

79
00:04:05,130 --> 00:04:06,930
moving the objects in the scene, back

80
00:04:06,930 --> 00:04:09,730
to some set of initial conditions

81
00:04:09,730 --> 00:04:14,770
And as a result, this process isn't actually fully autonomous

82
00:04:14,770 --> 00:04:18,570
So on the slides, we'll execute actions from our policy

83
00:04:18,570 --> 00:04:21,350
observe data from the environment, update our policy

84
00:04:21,350 --> 00:04:25,730
repeat that process, and eventually, we'll

85
00:04:25,730 --> 00:04:29,070
get a policy that is able to get high reward

86
00:04:29,070 --> 00:04:31,330
For example, in this problem, reaching a state

87
00:04:31,330 --> 00:04:35,610
within some region of goal states

88
00:04:35,610 --> 00:04:38,010
And we think about that as rolling out

89
00:04:38,010 --> 00:04:42,050
some trajectory of length h and then rolling out

90
00:04:42,050 --> 00:04:46,230
another trajectory of length h and so forth

91
00:04:46,230 --> 00:04:47,650
But we need to think about, how do

92
00:04:47,650 --> 00:04:50,930
we get from the end of one trajectory

93
00:04:50,930 --> 00:04:54,730
to the start of another trajectory

94
00:04:54,730 --> 00:04:58,720
Now, if we're in a simulation-based environment

95
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:01,180
then what we can do is we can create the environment

96
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:03,640
take actions for a thousand time steps

97
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:06,200
And then once we're done, then we

98
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:10,280
can call env.reset, which is a very

99
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:12,960
nice little friendly utility

100
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:15,540
But that's something that we can only really do in simulation

101
00:05:15,540 --> 00:05:22,840
We can't reset the physical world to our own will

102
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:25,640
And so as some specific examples

103
00:05:25,640 --> 00:05:29,080
say that you are trying to learn how to navigate to a basketball

104
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:29,580
court

105
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:31,310
Maybe you have a small mobile robot

106
00:05:31,310 --> 00:05:33,520
that is trying to deliver something to the basketball

107
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:35,280
court, or an autonomous vehicle that's

108
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:38,520
trying to navigate to the basketball court

109
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:42,120
As it tries to explore and attempt this task

110
00:05:42,120 --> 00:05:44,640
it might make a wrong turn, and rather

111
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:46,640
if it makes that wrong turn, it ends up

112
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:49,200
in that physical location and would

113
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:51,580
need to, for example, figure out how to navigate

114
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:53,080
either back to the starting position

115
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:56,040
or find its way from that wrong turn

116
00:05:56,040 --> 00:06:01,080
back to where the basketball truly is

117
00:06:01,080 --> 00:06:02,760
If we have a robot manipulator, we

118
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:04,680
might want to learn how to grasp the mug

119
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:07,240
As we're learning how to grasp the mug

120
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:10,880
the robot might make a mistake and knock the mug over

121
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:13,120
And then, if it wants to attempt and learn

122
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:16,400
how to grasp things again, then, somehow, it

123
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:20,520
needs to be within reach of the robot

124
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:24,280
Or for more of a mobile system, and we

125
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:28,040
want to learn how to put books onto bookshelves, again

126
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:31,320
we might make some mistakes and need

127
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:37,080
to figure out how to get back to some starting position, where

128
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:40,020
the book is in reach or some initial state distribution

129
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:43,120
if our goal is to go from one set of initial states

130
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:47,480
to regions with high reward

131
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:51,240
And one of the reasons why this is actually especially important

132
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:54,810
is that reinforcement learning often takes many attempts

133
00:06:54,810 --> 00:06:57,950
It's not a matter of taking trying three times

134
00:06:57,950 --> 00:06:59,510
and then solving the task

135
00:06:59,510 --> 00:07:01,430
Oftentimes, there's many different trials

136
00:07:01,430 --> 00:07:04,470
that you'll be attempting and practicing over and over again

137
00:07:04,470 --> 00:07:07,610
And every time the agent tries again

138
00:07:07,610 --> 00:07:10,390
that means that you have some human intervention

139
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:13,390
or some other intervention that is bringing you back

140
00:07:13,390 --> 00:07:16,110
to the initial state distribution

141
00:07:16,110 --> 00:07:21,630
As more of real examples, this video on the left

142
00:07:21,630 --> 00:07:25,590
is a video of a robot, learning how to hit a hockey

143
00:07:25,590 --> 00:07:26,730
puck into a goal

144
00:07:26,730 --> 00:07:30,470
And you can see that the human here-- this

145
00:07:30,470 --> 00:07:33,030
is a friend of mine, Yevgen-- he's probably

146
00:07:33,030 --> 00:07:35,790
doing more work than the robot is right now

147
00:07:35,790 --> 00:07:38,510
as it attempts to do this

148
00:07:38,510 --> 00:07:40,070
Here's another example, where Ali

149
00:07:40,070 --> 00:07:42,630
is closing the doors so that the robot can

150
00:07:42,630 --> 00:07:44,710
learn how to open them

151
00:07:44,710 --> 00:07:46,910
And here's an example where Archit

152
00:07:46,910 --> 00:07:50,990
who you gave a guest lecture earlier this quarter

153
00:07:50,990 --> 00:07:52,820
is resetting the robot so that it

154
00:07:52,820 --> 00:08:00,420
can attempt to fold the towel

155
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:05,180
OK, so how do we think about this problem

156
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:07,540
The problem is reset, often in practice

157
00:08:07,540 --> 00:08:09,660
requires some human intervention

158
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:12,040
that we want to be able to ideally eliminate

159
00:08:12,040 --> 00:08:15,360
so that our agents can practice and get better on their own

160
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:20,210
rather than requiring some expert or some human

161
00:08:20,210 --> 00:08:22,040
Now, one way that we could look at this is--

162
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:24,280
I mean, what if we could just increase h

163
00:08:24,280 --> 00:08:25,740
What if we just increase the length

164
00:08:25,740 --> 00:08:30,060
of the episodes in which our agents are attempting the task

165
00:08:30,060 --> 00:08:32,880
And if we increase the length of the episodes, then in principle

166
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:36,280
we won't require as much human supervision

167
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:38,700
And we might be able to apply the same exact reinforcement

168
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:43,020
learning algorithms that we've learned about in the course

169
00:08:43,020 --> 00:08:49,580
just with less supervision during the learning process

170
00:08:49,580 --> 00:08:50,970
So we can do that

171
00:08:50,970 --> 00:08:55,370
And so the we'll see an example of what increasing age

172
00:08:55,370 --> 00:08:56,650
looks like in a minute

173
00:08:56,650 --> 00:08:59,570
But generally, the definition of what we want to accomplish

174
00:08:59,570 --> 00:09:02,790
is initialize the scene, the environment

175
00:09:02,790 --> 00:09:05,010
Maybe it's already initialized in some way

176
00:09:05,010 --> 00:09:06,370
Then, we do that once

177
00:09:06,370 --> 00:09:10,290
We have the agent interact autonomously with the world

178
00:09:10,290 --> 00:09:12,810
without any environment resets

179
00:09:12,810 --> 00:09:15,090
We could also relax this constraint occasionally

180
00:09:15,090 --> 00:09:17,270
to only reset at a very low frequency

181
00:09:17,270 --> 00:09:20,290
For example, if h is very large

182
00:09:20,290 --> 00:09:23,630
Now, as we start to think about these systems

183
00:09:23,630 --> 00:09:25,210
we need to think about how we actually

184
00:09:25,210 --> 00:09:27,710
want to evaluate these systems

185
00:09:27,710 --> 00:09:29,630
And there's actually a couple different ways--

186
00:09:29,630 --> 00:09:31,380
there's actually a couple different things

187
00:09:31,380 --> 00:09:32,530
that we might care about

188
00:09:32,530 --> 00:09:35,410
So one thing that we might care about

189
00:09:35,410 --> 00:09:39,410
is after, for example, autonomously

190
00:09:39,410 --> 00:09:44,850
learned in some environment, we might care about--

191
00:09:44,850 --> 00:09:51,570
maybe let's-- well, let's say that we have autonomously

192
00:09:51,570 --> 00:09:52,530
explored

193
00:09:52,530 --> 00:09:59,970
And for example, we have some experience that we've collected

194
00:09:59,970 --> 00:10:02,550
Maybe that looks something like this

195
00:10:02,550 --> 00:10:06,810
where there's no resets, no going back to the initial state

196
00:10:06,810 --> 00:10:09,490
One thing that we might care about is we might care about

197
00:10:09,490 --> 00:10:13,650
did you achieve high reward as you went

198
00:10:13,650 --> 00:10:16,190
about the agent's experience

199
00:10:16,190 --> 00:10:18,650
And basically, did it actually complete the task at the end

200
00:10:18,650 --> 00:10:20,830
of the day, as it was learning

201
00:10:20,830 --> 00:10:22,250
And that would be looking at, what

202
00:10:22,250 --> 00:10:28,230
is the sum of the rewards that accomplished over its lifetime

203
00:10:28,230 --> 00:10:29,810
And alternatively, instead of thinking

204
00:10:29,810 --> 00:10:32,630
about evaluating autonomous RL from that perspective

205
00:10:32,630 --> 00:10:35,490
we could think about after the learning process

206
00:10:35,490 --> 00:10:38,530
did we learn a good policy that we could then deploy and be

207
00:10:38,530 --> 00:10:40,690
able to repeatedly do the task

208
00:10:40,690 --> 00:10:43,530
And so that would be looking at if we-- instead of looking

209
00:10:43,530 --> 00:10:45,230
at the rewards of this red trajectory

210
00:10:45,230 --> 00:10:47,150
it would be saying after the learning process

211
00:10:47,150 --> 00:10:51,420
if we've learned some policy, how good is it at

212
00:10:51,420 --> 00:10:54,720
for example, going to the goal

213
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:57,320
How good is the policy that it learned

214
00:10:57,320 --> 00:11:01,640
as an outcome of the reinforcement learning process

215
00:11:01,640 --> 00:11:03,780
So two things here

216
00:11:03,780 --> 00:11:06,400
One could be the amount of reward recovered

217
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:08,560
over the course of its life

218
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:11,760
As one example of this, imagine that you're deploying the Mars

219
00:11:11,760 --> 00:11:16,960
Rover, and you want the Rover to be able to collect

220
00:11:16,960 --> 00:11:18,880
scientific data

221
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:23,080
You don't really care about whether it learned a good policy

222
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:27,700
that you're going to deploy after the learning process

223
00:11:27,700 --> 00:11:29,200
You really care about whether or not

224
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:33,680
it did a good job, as it was deployed, as it was learning

225
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:34,920
and so forth

226
00:11:34,920 --> 00:11:37,760
So that's more like trying to measure how

227
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:42,960
good is this red trajectory

228
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:48,910
And so here's my graphical depiction of this on the slide

229
00:11:48,910 --> 00:11:52,170
Or you could learn about the quality of the learn policy

230
00:11:52,170 --> 00:11:53,670
An example of this is that if you're

231
00:11:53,670 --> 00:11:55,630
learning how to cook meals, maybe

232
00:11:55,630 --> 00:11:57,670
there's some training process

233
00:11:57,670 --> 00:11:59,670
And you don't really care that much about

234
00:11:59,670 --> 00:12:02,070
during the training process, how well it

235
00:12:02,070 --> 00:12:04,470
was able to cook as it was learning

236
00:12:04,470 --> 00:12:07,110
But then you really care about, then can I deploy this system

237
00:12:07,110 --> 00:12:12,390
and have it be able to cook meals effectively, repeatedly

238
00:12:12,390 --> 00:12:17,730
for a variety of customers in the future

239
00:12:17,730 --> 00:12:18,230
Cool

240
00:12:18,230 --> 00:12:20,350
So that would be typical

241
00:12:20,350 --> 00:12:24,550
If we then took a policy that we learned, initialized it

242
00:12:24,550 --> 00:12:27,870
at the starting state and ran it

243
00:12:27,870 --> 00:12:32,950
does it achieve good reward under those conditions

244
00:12:32,950 --> 00:12:35,290
So we'll think about these as two different things

245
00:12:35,290 --> 00:12:40,630
So we can-- first, let's think about what we'll call

246
00:12:40,630 --> 00:12:42,450
deployed policy evaluation

247
00:12:42,450 --> 00:12:44,350
So how good is the policy that we learned

248
00:12:44,350 --> 00:12:46,580
if we were to then deploy it

249
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:49,300
And the objective for this circumstance

250
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:53,900
can be defined as an objective that looks very similar to what

251
00:12:53,900 --> 00:12:57,060
we've been seeing in the course previously

252
00:12:57,060 --> 00:13:01,140
which is that you would sample a state from your initial state

253
00:13:01,140 --> 00:13:06,260
distribution and act according to your policy

254
00:13:06,260 --> 00:13:12,500
and measure the sum of rewards, or the sum of discounted rewards

255
00:13:12,500 --> 00:13:17,900
that was observed after running an episode

256
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:23,540
This should be an expectation after starting from the state

257
00:13:23,540 --> 00:13:27,580
and acting according to the policy

258
00:13:27,580 --> 00:13:30,600
So this is deployed policy evaluation

259
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:33,180
How good is the policy if it's starting from the initial state

260
00:13:33,180 --> 00:13:37,740
distribution and acting according to the policy

261
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:41,180
Alternatively, if we were to think about more

262
00:13:41,180 --> 00:13:44,500
the continuing policy evaluation, how good are

263
00:13:44,500 --> 00:13:48,300
if we just deploy the system, and it's trying to learn

264
00:13:48,300 --> 00:13:52,470
as it goes, and how good is it during that learning process

265
00:13:52,470 --> 00:13:54,260
Then we'd actually be considering something

266
00:13:54,260 --> 00:13:56,700
a little bit different

267
00:13:56,700 --> 00:14:00,140
We would want to be considering what

268
00:14:00,140 --> 00:14:06,180
is the average reward that it saw during that learning

269
00:14:06,180 --> 00:14:07,460
process

270
00:14:07,460 --> 00:14:17,480
And so we'd be looking at something some average quantity

271
00:14:17,480 --> 00:14:27,780
So we'll write this as 1/h of t equals 1 to h of the reward

272
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:34,260
And if h is small, this is not going

273
00:14:34,260 --> 00:14:35,920
to be averaged over small things

274
00:14:35,920 --> 00:14:39,860
And so we want to be evaluating this, usually

275
00:14:39,860 --> 00:14:43,160
as our lifetime might be getting longer and longer

276
00:14:43,160 --> 00:14:47,970
So this would be a limit, evaluating

277
00:14:47,970 --> 00:14:53,130
average reward and the limit in which h goes to infinity

278
00:14:53,130 --> 00:14:56,530
So something like this

279
00:14:56,530 --> 00:15:02,110
And there should be an expectation over this quantity

280
00:15:02,110 --> 00:15:07,290
Now, this expectation isn't under a single policy

281
00:15:07,290 --> 00:15:10,730
It's over the learning process itself

282
00:15:10,730 --> 00:15:13,290
So that's another difference from measuring

283
00:15:13,290 --> 00:15:17,370
how good a learned policy is

284
00:15:17,370 --> 00:15:17,970
Cool

285
00:15:17,970 --> 00:15:20,830
And so we'll look at algorithms for both of these circumstances

286
00:15:20,830 --> 00:15:22,290
Yeah, so the question is, why do we

287
00:15:22,290 --> 00:15:27,210
need an expectation if we're just running one trajectory

288
00:15:27,210 --> 00:15:33,610
If you want to evaluate, just a single lifetime

289
00:15:33,610 --> 00:15:37,430
or a single rollout of your learning process

290
00:15:37,430 --> 00:15:41,450
then you could just measure precisely that

291
00:15:41,450 --> 00:15:43,780
run it and see how well it does

292
00:15:43,780 --> 00:15:46,260
Now, in practice, there's variability

293
00:15:46,260 --> 00:15:48,260
that's caused by variability in the environment

294
00:15:48,260 --> 00:15:50,120
variability in your learning process

295
00:15:50,120 --> 00:15:52,560
like how you sample batches and so forth

296
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:56,540
And you may want to measure, like at the algorithm level

297
00:15:56,540 --> 00:16:00,600
how good is your algorithm at this problem

298
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:03,640
And if you want to measure how good the algorithm is

299
00:16:03,640 --> 00:16:08,660
you want an expectation over multiple runs of the algorithm

300
00:16:08,660 --> 00:16:09,580
Why is this important

301
00:16:09,580 --> 00:16:12,240
We've already talked a bit about the motivation

302
00:16:12,240 --> 00:16:16,120
but to elaborate on a little bit more

303
00:16:16,120 --> 00:16:19,350
we want robots to operate with minimal human supervision

304
00:16:19,350 --> 00:16:21,640
But we also want them to train with minimal supervision

305
00:16:21,640 --> 00:16:22,680
as well

306
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:27,400
And generally, reinforcement learning on real robots

307
00:16:27,400 --> 00:16:30,880
is a promising path towards achieving really high success

308
00:16:30,880 --> 00:16:34,680
rates, really high reliability compared to imitation learning

309
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:39,180
And if you're able to train with minimal human supervision

310
00:16:39,180 --> 00:16:41,200
then you're able to collect more data

311
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:44,840
and more data may also unlock both greater reliability

312
00:16:44,840 --> 00:16:48,400
and also greater generalization, if these systems are

313
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:52,280
able to be deployed at scale in a variety of real world

314
00:16:52,280 --> 00:16:54,520
environments

315
00:16:54,520 --> 00:16:56,520
So that's the problem setup

316
00:16:56,520 --> 00:17:00,120
Now, let's dive a bit into algorithms

317
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:05,980
So we'd like to learn policies without human supervision

318
00:17:05,980 --> 00:17:07,440
And we talked about this question

319
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:09,720
before of, what if we just increase

320
00:17:09,720 --> 00:17:13,520
the length of the episode

321
00:17:13,520 --> 00:17:15,599
And can we just take a traditional standard

322
00:17:15,599 --> 00:17:17,560
reinforcement learning algorithm

323
00:17:17,560 --> 00:17:21,160
In this case, I think we'll be looking at a soft actor-critic

324
00:17:21,160 --> 00:17:24,440
and just run it with longer and longer episodes, which

325
00:17:24,440 --> 00:17:29,480
in principle, should require less and less supervision

326
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:33,540
If we do this, we see performance changes a bit

327
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:36,480
So this is a plot that's evaluating

328
00:17:36,480 --> 00:17:41,030
how good is the learned policy after training for some number

329
00:17:41,030 --> 00:17:42,970
of steps, shown on the x-axis

330
00:17:42,970 --> 00:17:46,030
So the y-axis is reward, accomplished

331
00:17:46,030 --> 00:17:47,890
by the deployed policy

332
00:17:47,890 --> 00:17:52,950
So it's measuring this blue equation right here

333
00:17:52,950 --> 00:17:57,880
And this green line is if you run with an episode

334
00:17:57,880 --> 00:17:59,010
length of a thousand steps

335
00:17:59,010 --> 00:18:01,190
If you run SAC, you get a learning curve

336
00:18:01,190 --> 00:18:03,190
where it's able to learn a policy that's

337
00:18:03,190 --> 00:18:06,430
able to effectively complete the task

338
00:18:06,430 --> 00:18:09,390
Now, if you increase the length of the episodes

339
00:18:09,390 --> 00:18:12,390
during training by a factor of 2

340
00:18:12,390 --> 00:18:15,750
we see that the algorithm does worse

341
00:18:15,750 --> 00:18:18,630
and then we increase it by a factor of 10

342
00:18:18,630 --> 00:18:20,390
We see that it does even worse

343
00:18:20,390 --> 00:18:24,790
And basically, as we increase and make the episode length

344
00:18:24,790 --> 00:18:28,550
longer, and as a result, reduce the supervision required

345
00:18:28,550 --> 00:18:31,870
for training, the algorithm at the very end

346
00:18:31,870 --> 00:18:35,450
is hardly able to learn how to do the task at all

347
00:18:35,450 --> 00:18:39,140
how to learn a policy that can do the task at all

348
00:18:39,140 --> 00:18:43,540
And I should mention this is a very simple fish control

349
00:18:43,540 --> 00:18:47,820
task, where your goal is to navigate with what the fish can

350
00:18:47,820 --> 00:18:51,120
do to a particular position

351
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:52,580
So pretty simple task

352
00:18:52,580 --> 00:18:56,380
Even for the simple task, if you increase your episode length

353
00:18:56,380 --> 00:18:58,560
and don't give it resets very frequently

354
00:18:58,560 --> 00:19:01,660
you're aren't able to learn a good policy

355
00:19:01,660 --> 00:19:03,240
Now, why is this the case

356
00:19:03,240 --> 00:19:07,460
Does anyone have hypotheses for why we're not getting a good

357
00:19:07,460 --> 00:19:14,100
policy, if we only reset every 50,000 steps or 100,000 steps

358
00:19:14,100 --> 00:19:14,820
Yeah

359
00:19:14,820 --> 00:19:19,620
So the first issue that can happen is it can make a mistake

360
00:19:19,620 --> 00:19:22,460
and do something that's bad, maybe leads it

361
00:19:22,460 --> 00:19:24,380
to a part of the state space that

362
00:19:24,380 --> 00:19:26,470
is very hard to recover from

363
00:19:26,470 --> 00:19:27,850
In this particular environment, I

364
00:19:27,850 --> 00:19:30,520
don't think it's possible for it to break its tail or something

365
00:19:30,520 --> 00:19:34,060
but you can basically drift away from the state

366
00:19:34,060 --> 00:19:36,220
distribution that's good

367
00:19:36,220 --> 00:19:39,250
And so if we were to think about this

368
00:19:39,250 --> 00:19:41,450
maybe that diagram is getting a little bit messy

369
00:19:41,450 --> 00:19:43,770
Our goal is to learn a policy that

370
00:19:43,770 --> 00:19:48,270
goes from some initial state distribution to some goal

371
00:19:48,270 --> 00:19:50,390
as a simple reinforcement learning problem

372
00:19:50,390 --> 00:19:51,810
As we're learning, we might simply

373
00:19:51,810 --> 00:19:59,570
deviate from where the set of good states is

374
00:19:59,570 --> 00:20:01,730
And because of this drift, it might

375
00:20:01,730 --> 00:20:04,370
be very hard to recover and get closer

376
00:20:04,370 --> 00:20:07,930
to the parts that have high reward

377
00:20:07,930 --> 00:20:09,530
So that's one challenge

378
00:20:09,530 --> 00:20:13,650
Does anyone else have ideas on another challenge

379
00:20:13,650 --> 00:20:16,450
The other challenge is actually a little bit more nuanced

380
00:20:16,450 --> 00:20:19,470
and in particular, say that we drift away

381
00:20:19,470 --> 00:20:23,690
But maybe either in another episode after one reset

382
00:20:23,690 --> 00:20:30,090
or even for this episode, if you are able to eventually get

383
00:20:30,090 --> 00:20:31,470
to the goal, which is good

384
00:20:31,470 --> 00:20:34,170
You got reward

385
00:20:34,170 --> 00:20:40,810
From there, you might simply stay in this region of the state

386
00:20:40,810 --> 00:20:42,460
space

387
00:20:42,460 --> 00:20:44,170
And if you start collecting a lot of data

388
00:20:44,170 --> 00:20:47,010
in this part of the state space, you're getting lots of reward

389
00:20:47,010 --> 00:20:50,130
And so the learning process is great

390
00:20:50,130 --> 00:20:54,370
But then, if your goal is to learn a policy that

391
00:20:54,370 --> 00:20:56,410
starts from here and goes to here

392
00:20:56,410 --> 00:20:58,930
you're not actually visiting the states

393
00:20:58,930 --> 00:21:02,770
that will yield a good policy

394
00:21:02,770 --> 00:21:06,930
And so you might actually not be visiting the states that

395
00:21:06,930 --> 00:21:10,610
allow you to actually go effectively from one

396
00:21:10,610 --> 00:21:14,890
initial state to the goal

397
00:21:14,890 --> 00:21:18,650
So two challenges, if we think about this rho

398
00:21:18,650 --> 00:21:21,570
star as the optimal state distribution

399
00:21:21,570 --> 00:21:25,950
for getting from start to goal, then in an episodic learning

400
00:21:25,950 --> 00:21:28,530
you can always retry the task from the initial state

401
00:21:28,530 --> 00:21:30,250
distribution

402
00:21:30,250 --> 00:21:35,240
Whereas, in autonomous learning or non-episodic learning

403
00:21:35,240 --> 00:21:37,360
you'll continue from where you left off

404
00:21:37,360 --> 00:21:39,680
and that exploration might lead you

405
00:21:39,680 --> 00:21:43,840
to drift away from the set of good states for getting

406
00:21:43,840 --> 00:21:46,320
to the goal

407
00:21:46,320 --> 00:21:48,560
And then even if you do eventually get to the goal

408
00:21:48,560 --> 00:21:51,520
your state distribution in your replay buffer

409
00:21:51,520 --> 00:21:58,400
might collapse and might be concentrated around the goal

410
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:02,400
rather than capturing the state distribution that's

411
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:04,150
around the optimal policy

412
00:22:04,150 --> 00:22:06,360
And if you don't have states from the optimal policy

413
00:22:06,360 --> 00:22:08,960
you won't know what to do or what actions to take

414
00:22:08,960 --> 00:22:12,680
when you're in this part of the state space

415
00:22:12,680 --> 00:22:13,460
Cool

416
00:22:13,460 --> 00:22:14,680
So as an outcome of this, you'll never

417
00:22:14,680 --> 00:22:19,600
actually learn a good policy

418
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:24,360
So how can we learn an effective policy

419
00:22:24,360 --> 00:22:28,360
So if the agent is allowed to reset from the initial state

420
00:22:28,360 --> 00:22:31,200
distribution, we do learn successfully

421
00:22:31,200 --> 00:22:34,670
And so one very simple idea is to try

422
00:22:34,670 --> 00:22:39,870
to learn a policy that brings us from wherever we ended up

423
00:22:39,870 --> 00:22:43,070
after attempting the task back to the initial state

424
00:22:43,070 --> 00:22:45,010
distribution

425
00:22:45,010 --> 00:22:46,430
And we can call this idea-- we can

426
00:22:46,430 --> 00:22:50,030
think of this as a reset policy or a backwards policy that's

427
00:22:50,030 --> 00:22:56,310
trying to undo the task rather than do the task forward

428
00:22:56,310 --> 00:23:00,410
And so we can think of this as a forward backward RL

429
00:23:00,410 --> 00:23:03,310
because we will be learning a forward policy that

430
00:23:03,310 --> 00:23:07,870
does the task, and a backward policy that undoes the task

431
00:23:07,870 --> 00:23:14,190
And where to put this

432
00:23:14,190 --> 00:23:15,990
Maybe let's do it over here

433
00:23:15,990 --> 00:23:25,750
So you'll first-- maybe step 0 is to initialize and sample

434
00:23:25,750 --> 00:23:30,550
an initial state from your initial state distribution

435
00:23:30,550 --> 00:23:32,590
basically initializing the agent

436
00:23:32,590 --> 00:23:39,740
And then you're going to repeatedly run

437
00:23:39,740 --> 00:23:43,620
your forward policy, which I'll call pi f

438
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:49,780
then update your forward policy by trying

439
00:23:49,780 --> 00:23:53,400
to maximize the sum of rewards

440
00:23:53,400 --> 00:23:57,460
And this will be using the reward function

441
00:23:57,460 --> 00:23:59,180
for completing the task

442
00:23:59,180 --> 00:24:04,460
I'll call that rf, the forward-reward function

443
00:24:04,460 --> 00:24:08,500
And then we'll reach some state we won't reset

444
00:24:08,500 --> 00:24:13,820
and we'll run our backward policy, pi b

445
00:24:13,820 --> 00:24:20,540
And then we can likewise update our backward policy

446
00:24:20,540 --> 00:24:23,220
In this case, we need to update it

447
00:24:23,220 --> 00:24:25,600
for the reverse of the forward task

448
00:24:25,600 --> 00:24:28,060
And so we'll actually rely on a reward function that

449
00:24:28,060 --> 00:24:33,780
will tell us if we are reaching the initial state distribution

450
00:24:33,780 --> 00:24:36,260
And so this is going to require a second reward

451
00:24:36,260 --> 00:24:41,580
function, rb that is going to serve as reward

452
00:24:41,580 --> 00:24:43,780
for the backward policy

453
00:24:43,780 --> 00:24:45,240
Now in terms of these update steps

454
00:24:45,240 --> 00:24:47,740
you can use your favorite reinforcement

455
00:24:47,740 --> 00:24:50,900
learning algorithm for this

456
00:24:50,900 --> 00:24:54,300
And critically, after we run the backward policy

457
00:24:54,300 --> 00:24:55,980
we're not going to reset

458
00:24:55,980 --> 00:25:02,220
We'll then go back to running the forward policy again

459
00:25:02,220 --> 00:25:04,860
And so what this would look like is we

460
00:25:04,860 --> 00:25:07,440
will define this backward-reward function

461
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:12,660
which is rewards us for reaching states in the initial state

462
00:25:12,660 --> 00:25:14,220
distribution

463
00:25:14,220 --> 00:25:16,220
Then we'll initialize them and repeatedly

464
00:25:16,220 --> 00:25:20,140
roll out the forward policy, then update the forward policy

465
00:25:20,140 --> 00:25:22,100
with respect to reaching the goal

466
00:25:22,100 --> 00:25:24,420
Roll out the backward policy, which

467
00:25:24,420 --> 00:25:27,980
is going to try to go back to the initial state distribution

468
00:25:27,980 --> 00:25:30,890
then update the backward policy

469
00:25:30,890 --> 00:25:32,670
And then without resetting, again

470
00:25:32,670 --> 00:25:34,150
try to roll out the forward policy

471
00:25:34,150 --> 00:25:38,170
roll out the backward policy, and repeat this process

472
00:25:38,170 --> 00:25:42,330
You can also have a reset very infrequently, so occasionally

473
00:25:42,330 --> 00:25:44,970
reset it, just in case it does get stuck

474
00:25:44,970 --> 00:25:49,570
in a really challenging state

475
00:25:49,570 --> 00:25:52,590
And then at test time, once you're done with this process

476
00:25:52,590 --> 00:25:54,250
you can discard the backward policy

477
00:25:54,250 --> 00:25:56,370
and just deploy your forward policy, which

478
00:25:56,370 --> 00:25:58,570
should have learned, ideally, how

479
00:25:58,570 --> 00:26:02,810
to get efficiently from the initial state distribution

480
00:26:02,810 --> 00:26:06,370
to the goal, or generally, to maximize

481
00:26:06,370 --> 00:26:09,610
the forward-forward function

482
00:26:09,610 --> 00:26:11,990
One other small note here is that we're going to be

483
00:26:11,990 --> 00:26:14,130
Typically, you need to decide how long to roll out

484
00:26:14,130 --> 00:26:15,870
the forward policy for

485
00:26:15,870 --> 00:26:17,290
The simplest thing to do is simply

486
00:26:17,290 --> 00:26:20,010
to roll it out for h time steps, so

487
00:26:20,010 --> 00:26:22,930
choose a constant number of time steps to roll it out for

488
00:26:22,930 --> 00:26:25,770
You could imagine doing something more sophisticated

489
00:26:25,770 --> 00:26:28,950
for example, before the forward policy has learned anything

490
00:26:28,950 --> 00:26:31,420
the backward policy, if it's not moving anywhere

491
00:26:31,420 --> 00:26:33,500
the backward policy won't really have any job

492
00:26:33,500 --> 00:26:34,460
to do at the beginning

493
00:26:34,460 --> 00:26:36,090
And so you, for example, could run this

494
00:26:36,090 --> 00:26:38,040
for a little bit longer at the beginning

495
00:26:38,040 --> 00:26:39,200
but then at some point, you also need

496
00:26:39,200 --> 00:26:41,360
to run the backward policy for a little bit longer

497
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:46,440
And it's a bit simpler to just use a fixed number of steps

498
00:26:46,440 --> 00:26:48,520
And ideally, if this process is autonomous

499
00:26:48,520 --> 00:26:50,680
then the number of time steps actually

500
00:26:50,680 --> 00:26:52,800
matters a little bit less because you

501
00:26:52,800 --> 00:26:56,940
can go to sleep while your robot is trying to learn

502
00:26:56,940 --> 00:26:59,360
Yeah, so the question is, is there any information sharing

503
00:26:59,360 --> 00:27:02,200
between the forward policy and backward policy

504
00:27:02,200 --> 00:27:03,800
If you're using replay buffers, you

505
00:27:03,800 --> 00:27:06,000
could consider sharing data between the two

506
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:09,480
and relabeling the rewards for the forward policy for backward

507
00:27:09,480 --> 00:27:12,560
policy, and vice-versa

508
00:27:12,560 --> 00:27:15,480
In practice, it's fairly common to actually

509
00:27:15,480 --> 00:27:17,100
not share much information

510
00:27:17,100 --> 00:27:19,920
You can also share an image encoder, for example

511
00:27:19,920 --> 00:27:22,440
share some of the weights

512
00:27:22,440 --> 00:27:26,120
That can be helpful, but they are actually trying to learn

513
00:27:26,120 --> 00:27:27,950
in some ways, very different things

514
00:27:27,950 --> 00:27:29,660
And so that makes it a little bit tricky

515
00:27:29,660 --> 00:27:34,600
We'll also see some algorithms later on that do more sharing

516
00:27:34,600 --> 00:27:38,600
So the upside of this algorithm is quite simple

517
00:27:38,600 --> 00:27:41,520
One of the downsides is that this backward policy

518
00:27:41,520 --> 00:27:43,400
that's trying to undo the task

519
00:27:43,400 --> 00:27:48,040
Ultimately, this is not particularly useful

520
00:27:48,040 --> 00:27:51,320
So in some ways, it seems a little bit

521
00:27:51,320 --> 00:27:53,200
wasteful to learn this policy, but it

522
00:27:53,200 --> 00:27:58,720
does enable the autonomous learning which is quite nice

523
00:27:58,720 --> 00:28:03,800
So this is the most simple algorithm

524
00:28:03,800 --> 00:28:07,320
you could use for trying to learn autonomously

525
00:28:07,320 --> 00:28:10,400
But there are also some alternatives that, in some ways

526
00:28:10,400 --> 00:28:13,080
have a number of appealing properties

527
00:28:13,080 --> 00:28:16,570
So first, for your backward policy

528
00:28:16,570 --> 00:28:18,360
you can actually change the reward function

529
00:28:18,360 --> 00:28:22,520
that it uses to actually not reset all the way back

530
00:28:22,520 --> 00:28:24,000
to the beginning, but potentially

531
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:29,310
to reset from a larger variety of states

532
00:28:29,310 --> 00:28:32,310
Second, we could, instead of learning forward and backward

533
00:28:32,310 --> 00:28:34,070
just for one task, we could think

534
00:28:34,070 --> 00:28:37,950
about practicing a cycle of different tasks

535
00:28:37,950 --> 00:28:41,290
and not just learning a forward task and a backward task

536
00:28:41,290 --> 00:28:42,460
We'll talk about that a bit

537
00:28:42,460 --> 00:28:44,160
And then the other option is you can also

538
00:28:44,160 --> 00:28:46,910
set up a task that can be done repeatedly, where you can just

539
00:28:46,910 --> 00:28:50,190
keep on attempting the task over and over again

540
00:28:50,190 --> 00:28:52,500
in certain special circumstances

541
00:28:52,500 --> 00:28:56,730
And we'll talk about that briefly as well

542
00:28:56,730 --> 00:28:57,230
Cool

543
00:28:57,230 --> 00:28:59,030
Let's first talk about learning to reset

544
00:28:59,030 --> 00:29:01,330
to a different initial state distribution

545
00:29:01,330 --> 00:29:02,290
from the initial state

546
00:29:02,290 --> 00:29:06,750
And so the motivation here is that in forward-backward RL

547
00:29:06,750 --> 00:29:09,070
we're trying the task, going back, trying the task

548
00:29:09,070 --> 00:29:10,190
going back

549
00:29:10,190 --> 00:29:16,630
And ultimately, we want to be able to do complete

550
00:29:16,630 --> 00:29:18,950
the task from the initial state distribution

551
00:29:18,950 --> 00:29:21,350
but we also need to know how to take

552
00:29:21,350 --> 00:29:26,300
good actions, all along the expert state distribution as

553
00:29:26,300 --> 00:29:27,230
well

554
00:29:27,230 --> 00:29:28,940
And so one of the things that we could do

555
00:29:28,940 --> 00:29:30,440
is instead of going back all the way

556
00:29:30,440 --> 00:29:32,300
to the beginning, every single time

557
00:29:32,300 --> 00:29:35,700
we could actually reset to different states

558
00:29:35,700 --> 00:29:39,700
along the expert state distribution

559
00:29:39,700 --> 00:29:41,700
And there's actually work that shows

560
00:29:41,700 --> 00:29:44,840
that if you were to do this and had a way to do this

561
00:29:44,840 --> 00:29:46,860
then you'd actually learn a bit more efficiently

562
00:29:46,860 --> 00:29:50,200
than always starting from the initial state distribution

563
00:29:50,200 --> 00:29:52,780
even in episodic learning settings

564
00:29:52,780 --> 00:29:55,660
And so the way that this works is it's

565
00:29:55,660 --> 00:29:57,580
basically going to be the same exact thing

566
00:29:57,580 --> 00:30:04,740
as before, but with basically two small modifications

567
00:30:04,740 --> 00:30:07,980
And the first is that if we want to be able to reset and learn

568
00:30:07,980 --> 00:30:11,500
a policy that brings us to the expert state distribution, then

569
00:30:11,500 --> 00:30:13,820
we need a way to approximate what that expert state

570
00:30:13,820 --> 00:30:15,300
distribution is

571
00:30:15,300 --> 00:30:19,940
And so we'll assume that we have a few demonstrations

572
00:30:19,940 --> 00:30:27,130
And so maybe negative 1 here is you're basically

573
00:30:27,130 --> 00:30:33,850
given some set of expert trajectories or expert

574
00:30:33,850 --> 00:30:37,450
demonstrations, and these will be examples

575
00:30:37,450 --> 00:30:40,610
of ways to accomplish the task

576
00:30:40,610 --> 00:30:43,530
And then you need to change the reward function

577
00:30:43,530 --> 00:30:46,770
for the backward policy, where the reward function shouldn't

578
00:30:46,770 --> 00:30:50,030
be to reach the initial state distribution

579
00:30:50,030 --> 00:30:54,890
but instead, it should be to reach states

580
00:30:54,890 --> 00:30:58,570
that the expert visits

581
00:30:58,570 --> 00:31:00,450
And so in particular, we'll be given

582
00:31:00,450 --> 00:31:02,250
a small set of demonstrations

583
00:31:02,250 --> 00:31:04,330
And ideally, what we want this to look like is

584
00:31:04,330 --> 00:31:06,210
we'd run our forward policy

585
00:31:06,210 --> 00:31:08,890
And then when we run our backward policy

586
00:31:08,890 --> 00:31:12,290
after it's learned something reasonable

587
00:31:12,290 --> 00:31:14,210
ideally, it would just bring us back

588
00:31:14,210 --> 00:31:20,050
to the set of the set of states in the expert distribution

589
00:31:20,050 --> 00:31:21,740
Then we run our forward policy again

590
00:31:21,740 --> 00:31:23,370
and then it would bring us back, again

591
00:31:23,370 --> 00:31:25,490
somewhere in this pink region that

592
00:31:25,490 --> 00:31:27,550
is showing the distribution of states

593
00:31:27,550 --> 00:31:32,570
that are visited by the expert

594
00:31:32,570 --> 00:31:38,250
And so the forward-backward process is the same

595
00:31:38,250 --> 00:31:41,130
but we have this modified backward-reward function

596
00:31:41,130 --> 00:31:46,050
And we're actually going to learn this reward function

597
00:31:46,050 --> 00:31:50,330
And in particular, we talked about in the lecture

598
00:31:50,330 --> 00:31:55,730
on learning reward functions, how you can train discriminators

599
00:31:55,730 --> 00:31:59,730
to discriminate between good states and bad states

600
00:31:59,730 --> 00:32:05,290
and use states from the policy as examples of bad states

601
00:32:05,290 --> 00:32:07,430
And we can do the same exact thing here

602
00:32:07,430 --> 00:32:11,130
where this reward function will be trained to distinguish states

603
00:32:11,130 --> 00:32:16,210
visited by the policy and states visited in the demonstrations

604
00:32:16,210 --> 00:32:18,810
And then you'll be trying to-- when

605
00:32:18,810 --> 00:32:20,510
you're training the backward policy

606
00:32:20,510 --> 00:32:23,180
you'll be trying to trick this reward function into thinking

607
00:32:23,180 --> 00:32:24,640
that it's the state that's visiting

608
00:32:24,640 --> 00:32:29,670
our states from the expert and not from the policy

609
00:32:29,670 --> 00:32:31,800
And then, again, we'll discard our backward policy

610
00:32:31,800 --> 00:32:38,340
when we're done, and deploy our forward policy

611
00:32:38,340 --> 00:32:38,840
Cool

612
00:32:38,840 --> 00:32:40,210
So let's see some demonstrations

613
00:32:40,210 --> 00:32:43,760
run our forward policy, backward policy, and so forth

614
00:32:43,760 --> 00:32:47,840
This forward-backward RL addresses the challenge

615
00:32:47,840 --> 00:32:50,400
of preventing the agent from drifting away

616
00:32:50,400 --> 00:32:54,760
And it also avoids collapsing the distribution that we

617
00:32:54,760 --> 00:32:59,240
talked about before as well

618
00:32:59,240 --> 00:32:59,740
Cool

619
00:32:59,740 --> 00:33:02,320
And then the upside that I mentioned very briefly

620
00:33:02,320 --> 00:33:06,880
is that if you try the task from a wide set of initial states

621
00:33:06,880 --> 00:33:09,240
both easier states, like states that

622
00:33:09,240 --> 00:33:11,840
are closer to the goal and further states, then

623
00:33:11,840 --> 00:33:14,040
you could actually improve the efficiency

624
00:33:14,040 --> 00:33:17,510
of the entire reinforcement learning algorithm

625
00:33:17,510 --> 00:33:19,880
The last thing that I'll mention is that you can actually

626
00:33:19,880 --> 00:33:24,510
use these demonstrations in the replay buffers

627
00:33:24,510 --> 00:33:27,070
as data to help train the forward policy

628
00:33:27,070 --> 00:33:29,750
and the reverse policy as well

629
00:33:29,750 --> 00:33:32,870
So the question is, if the agent finishes the task

630
00:33:32,870 --> 00:33:35,430
does the backward policy just stay there

631
00:33:35,430 --> 00:33:38,330
And here the answer is no

632
00:33:38,330 --> 00:33:40,670
So in the case of forward-backward RL

633
00:33:40,670 --> 00:33:42,290
it would go back to the initial state

634
00:33:42,290 --> 00:33:46,450
Whereas, in the case of this MEDAL algorithm

635
00:33:46,450 --> 00:33:50,790
it'll try to push the policy to a state in the expert state

636
00:33:50,790 --> 00:33:52,110
distribution

637
00:33:52,110 --> 00:33:56,870
And specifically when we're training the classifier

638
00:33:56,870 --> 00:34:00,610
to classify between policy states and expert states

639
00:34:00,610 --> 00:34:03,630
we're using all of the states in the trajectories, all

640
00:34:03,630 --> 00:34:05,430
the states in the demos

641
00:34:05,430 --> 00:34:09,010
And so it's going to, for example, like in this graphic

642
00:34:09,010 --> 00:34:12,310
go back to somewhere over here so that the policy then

643
00:34:12,310 --> 00:34:15,650
learns and practices how to do the task from here

644
00:34:15,650 --> 00:34:19,150
rather than only practicing it from the goal state

645
00:34:19,150 --> 00:34:20,650
The question is, what prevents it

646
00:34:20,650 --> 00:34:22,320
from actually staying in the goal state

647
00:34:22,320 --> 00:34:25,380
Because the goal state is part of the demonstrations

648
00:34:25,380 --> 00:34:28,570
The specifics of--

649
00:34:28,570 --> 00:34:30,280
I was thinking about not going into this

650
00:34:30,280 --> 00:34:32,860
But basically, the specifics of this reward function

651
00:34:32,860 --> 00:34:36,659
is actually a state distribution matching objective

652
00:34:36,659 --> 00:34:42,699
where it's goal is to match the states that it visits

653
00:34:42,699 --> 00:34:44,500
with the states and the demos

654
00:34:44,500 --> 00:34:47,880
And if it only resets to the goal state

655
00:34:47,880 --> 00:34:50,620
then the classifier or the discriminator of this reward

656
00:34:50,620 --> 00:34:53,179
function will learn that if you're only in the goal state

657
00:34:53,179 --> 00:34:55,300
then that's probably coming from the policy and not

658
00:34:55,300 --> 00:34:57,220
from the expert demos

659
00:34:57,220 --> 00:35:01,180
And it will be able to distinguish that

660
00:35:01,180 --> 00:35:03,700
And the way that you can formalize that is you

661
00:35:03,700 --> 00:35:06,380
can actually think about it as a divergence

662
00:35:06,380 --> 00:35:09,020
between the distribution of states

663
00:35:09,020 --> 00:35:11,740
visited by the backward policy and the distribution of states

664
00:35:11,740 --> 00:35:16,060
visited by the experts

665
00:35:16,060 --> 00:35:16,740
Cool

666
00:35:16,740 --> 00:35:21,940
So let's actually briefly look at how these algorithms perform

667
00:35:21,940 --> 00:35:27,660
So there's actually a benchmark for this autonomous

668
00:35:27,660 --> 00:35:30,740
reinforcement learning setting called EARL

669
00:35:30,740 --> 00:35:35,460
And during training, you only get a reset every 200,000

670
00:35:35,460 --> 00:35:38,580
environment steps, so very infrequently

671
00:35:38,580 --> 00:35:41,500
And the evaluation is, how good is the policy

672
00:35:41,500 --> 00:35:45,330
when you start it from some initial state distribution

673
00:35:45,330 --> 00:35:47,000
The benchmark has a few different tasks

674
00:35:47,000 --> 00:35:48,420
This is probably the simplest task

675
00:35:48,420 --> 00:35:53,340
in the benchmark, where you can basically try to grab a cup

676
00:35:53,340 --> 00:35:54,620
and move it

677
00:35:54,620 --> 00:35:58,140
And there's this very simple grabbing mechanism

678
00:35:58,140 --> 00:36:00,220
that makes the task simpler

679
00:36:00,220 --> 00:36:02,900
And then we'll also look at a more complex task

680
00:36:02,900 --> 00:36:06,620
on the next slide

681
00:36:06,620 --> 00:36:09,340
And if we look at what different algorithms do in this

682
00:36:09,340 --> 00:36:13,740
environment, where they only get resets every 200,000 steps--

683
00:36:13,740 --> 00:36:19,090
First, if we just run SAC without a backward policy, where

684
00:36:19,090 --> 00:36:20,710
it only gets very infrequent resets

685
00:36:20,710 --> 00:36:23,170
like we saw with the fish, the performance isn't very good

686
00:36:23,170 --> 00:36:27,490
and it doesn't learn a good forward policy

687
00:36:27,490 --> 00:36:33,090
If we use an Oracle that is able to get resets

688
00:36:33,090 --> 00:36:34,770
much more frequently than it is actually

689
00:36:34,770 --> 00:36:37,730
able to learn a good policy

690
00:36:37,730 --> 00:36:41,890
And then there's a few reset-free algorithms

691
00:36:41,890 --> 00:36:44,330
One is something that tries to perturb it, and encourage

692
00:36:44,330 --> 00:36:46,330
it to visit novel states

693
00:36:46,330 --> 00:36:49,970
Another, showed in orange, is the vanilla forward-backward

694
00:36:49,970 --> 00:36:53,010
reinforcement learning algorithm

695
00:36:53,010 --> 00:36:55,650
We can also give it some goal curriculum

696
00:36:55,650 --> 00:36:58,370
I didn't talk specifically about this algorithm

697
00:36:58,370 --> 00:37:00,690
And then the blue algorithm is the algorithm

698
00:37:00,690 --> 00:37:07,170
that's trying to reset back to states in the demonstrations

699
00:37:07,170 --> 00:37:10,530
And so we see that, first, I guess the main takeaway here

700
00:37:10,530 --> 00:37:14,330
is that these algorithms that are specifically designed

701
00:37:14,330 --> 00:37:17,840
for this autonomous or reset-free learning setting

702
00:37:17,840 --> 00:37:20,960
are able to do much better than if you don't design it

703
00:37:20,960 --> 00:37:22,800
for that case

704
00:37:22,800 --> 00:37:24,680
And in this simple environment, they all

705
00:37:24,680 --> 00:37:27,720
perform fairly similarly

706
00:37:27,720 --> 00:37:31,600
And then as we move towards more complex environments

707
00:37:31,600 --> 00:37:35,720
we see that forward-backward RL and MEDAL

708
00:37:35,720 --> 00:37:38,340
are able to do the best

709
00:37:38,340 --> 00:37:39,800
Whereas, simply, if you instead try

710
00:37:39,800 --> 00:37:44,680
to encourage it to visit various states or goals

711
00:37:44,680 --> 00:37:47,640
those algorithms aren't doing quite as well

712
00:37:47,640 --> 00:37:50,760
Yeah, the question is, we're you assuming some expert

713
00:37:50,760 --> 00:37:54,400
trajectories, how are those generated

714
00:37:54,400 --> 00:37:58,300
So there's a variety of ways that you can generate them

715
00:37:58,300 --> 00:37:59,800
On a real robot, you would typically

716
00:37:59,800 --> 00:38:03,080
use some teleoperation device

717
00:38:03,080 --> 00:38:09,160
And that could be using a puppeteering-like setup or a VR

718
00:38:09,160 --> 00:38:11,760
controller or a 3D mouse or a keyboard

719
00:38:11,760 --> 00:38:14,440
In simulation, it can either be something

720
00:38:14,440 --> 00:38:18,890
that's like a scripted policy that is designed by a person

721
00:38:18,890 --> 00:38:23,030
or it could be a policy that's trained with RL ahead of time

722
00:38:23,030 --> 00:38:25,990
just to test the algorithms

723
00:38:25,990 --> 00:38:28,490
Can I describe what the episodic part is

724
00:38:28,490 --> 00:38:31,350
So episodic is a term that often refers

725
00:38:31,350 --> 00:38:33,190
to the traditional reinforcement learning

726
00:38:33,190 --> 00:38:35,910
setting, where we can attempt the task

727
00:38:35,910 --> 00:38:37,930
and reset and attempt the task again

728
00:38:37,930 --> 00:38:40,290
where each attempt is like an episode

729
00:38:40,290 --> 00:38:41,370
And so it's very episodic

730
00:38:41,370 --> 00:38:42,450
You get one episode

731
00:38:42,450 --> 00:38:44,630
You try again, you get another episode

732
00:38:44,630 --> 00:38:48,570
And then it's not the best term, but that's one term

733
00:38:48,570 --> 00:38:51,350
and then non-episodic refers to the setting

734
00:38:51,350 --> 00:38:53,150
where you don't get those resets

735
00:38:53,150 --> 00:38:54,390
You just let it go

736
00:38:54,390 --> 00:38:57,210
And so it's no longer a bunch of episodes

737
00:38:57,210 --> 00:39:01,800
It's just like one long learning process

738
00:39:01,800 --> 00:39:03,470
Sometimes, that learning process is also

739
00:39:03,470 --> 00:39:08,220
called reset-free because there are no resets in the process

740
00:39:08,220 --> 00:39:10,390
Oh, the other thing that I should mention, actually

741
00:39:10,390 --> 00:39:12,410
that's cool about this particular task

742
00:39:12,410 --> 00:39:15,150
is the forward task is to close the door

743
00:39:15,150 --> 00:39:17,010
and the backward task is to open the door

744
00:39:17,010 --> 00:39:18,750
And the backward task is, actually

745
00:39:18,750 --> 00:39:20,750
depending on where the arm starts

746
00:39:20,750 --> 00:39:23,670
it can actually be quite a bit harder than the forward task

747
00:39:23,670 --> 00:39:25,750
And so you could, sometimes, have these mismatches

748
00:39:25,750 --> 00:39:28,070
between how hard it is to do the forward task

749
00:39:28,070 --> 00:39:29,910
and how hard it is to do the backward task

750
00:39:29,910 --> 00:39:32,790
And that can make these algorithms-- that can generally

751
00:39:32,790 --> 00:39:35,650
sometimes, make the learning process a bit more challenging

752
00:39:35,650 --> 00:39:37,790
Because the forward policy, the backward policy

753
00:39:37,790 --> 00:39:40,590
will learn at different rates

754
00:39:40,590 --> 00:39:41,970
Similarly, if you could imagine--

755
00:39:41,970 --> 00:39:44,490
if your forward task is to do a backflip

756
00:39:44,490 --> 00:39:47,490
for example, maybe doing a backflip is really challenging

757
00:39:47,490 --> 00:39:50,030
but getting up and standing up to start doing the backflip

758
00:39:50,030 --> 00:39:52,350
is comparably easier

759
00:39:52,350 --> 00:39:55,030
And so it's fairly common to have

760
00:39:55,030 --> 00:39:58,850
a mismatch in that difficulty

761
00:39:58,850 --> 00:40:00,870
These algorithms have also--

762
00:40:00,870 --> 00:40:03,610
you can actually run them on real robots, and it's cool

763
00:40:03,610 --> 00:40:06,230
You can watch time lapses of them

764
00:40:06,230 --> 00:40:08,870
The left video is running forward-backward RL

765
00:40:08,870 --> 00:40:10,410
where it's learning a forward policy

766
00:40:10,410 --> 00:40:11,810
learning a backward policy

767
00:40:11,810 --> 00:40:15,620
And you can see that both policies make mistakes

768
00:40:15,620 --> 00:40:18,020
and it's able to--

769
00:40:18,020 --> 00:40:20,100
I don't know if you can see it in the context

770
00:40:20,100 --> 00:40:23,020
of this single video, but it is able to improve just

771
00:40:23,020 --> 00:40:26,920
through a fully autonomous process

772
00:40:26,920 --> 00:40:30,040
And then the right is the metal algorithm

773
00:40:30,040 --> 00:40:31,900
And you can see that, for example

774
00:40:31,900 --> 00:40:34,780
if the forward policy misses, then the backward policy

775
00:40:34,780 --> 00:40:36,420
doesn't really have much to do

776
00:40:36,420 --> 00:40:40,260
And so you do see, yeah, that another property

777
00:40:40,260 --> 00:40:46,020
of the algorithm in the video

778
00:40:46,020 --> 00:40:47,050
Cool

779
00:40:47,050 --> 00:40:48,420
One other thing that I'll mention

780
00:40:48,420 --> 00:40:51,520
is that rewards are also something that provide

781
00:40:51,520 --> 00:40:53,940
And so using reward learning algorithms

782
00:40:53,940 --> 00:40:58,260
in conjunction with autonomous or reset-free algorithms

783
00:40:58,260 --> 00:41:01,080
is also quite common, and in both of these circumstances

784
00:41:01,080 --> 00:41:04,500
the rewards were using models rather than using humans

785
00:41:04,500 --> 00:41:07,380
So that this entire process can be autonomous

786
00:41:07,380 --> 00:41:12,130
and you can actually run it overnight, for example

787
00:41:12,130 --> 00:41:14,230
Yeah, so the question is, it seems

788
00:41:14,230 --> 00:41:15,730
like for some of these tasks, if you

789
00:41:15,730 --> 00:41:17,210
don't have a good backward policy

790
00:41:17,210 --> 00:41:19,290
then it's really hard to learn the forward policy

791
00:41:19,290 --> 00:41:20,810
And also, vice-versa, if you don't

792
00:41:20,810 --> 00:41:23,150
have a good forward policy, then you're

793
00:41:23,150 --> 00:41:25,150
never actually going to be picking up the towel

794
00:41:25,150 --> 00:41:29,530
And then you need to figure out how to--

795
00:41:29,530 --> 00:41:33,140
then you're not really getting actually any good states

796
00:41:33,140 --> 00:41:34,810
In general, one of the things you can do

797
00:41:34,810 --> 00:41:36,270
is, you can provide demonstrations

798
00:41:36,270 --> 00:41:38,760
both for the forward task and for the backward task

799
00:41:38,760 --> 00:41:40,890
and use that to help bootstrap the learning process

800
00:41:40,890 --> 00:41:43,410
for both of them

801
00:41:43,410 --> 00:41:46,410
Other than that, it is just generally

802
00:41:46,410 --> 00:41:53,170
a challenge and to try to balance these two things

803
00:41:53,170 --> 00:41:56,010
Yeah, in general, the algorithms are

804
00:41:56,010 --> 00:41:58,690
able to work in some settings

805
00:41:58,690 --> 00:42:01,200
I should also mention that these works--

806
00:42:01,200 --> 00:42:06,280
most that are doing these, they're still--

807
00:42:06,280 --> 00:42:08,650
I mean, certainly, still in the research stages, I think

808
00:42:08,650 --> 00:42:13,810
This is not something that robot companies are using today

809
00:42:13,810 --> 00:42:15,490
Yeah

810
00:42:15,490 --> 00:42:16,530
Cool

811
00:42:16,530 --> 00:42:18,250
So we've talked about algorithms that

812
00:42:18,250 --> 00:42:22,330
learn a reset policy to help learning a forward policy

813
00:42:22,330 --> 00:42:27,210
Now, we can also think about a slightly different setting

814
00:42:27,210 --> 00:42:30,090
which is that if we're trying to learn, not just one task

815
00:42:30,090 --> 00:42:33,730
but multiple tasks, then we could actually

816
00:42:33,730 --> 00:42:36,830
think about not just going forward and backward

817
00:42:36,830 --> 00:42:41,010
but practicing going in cycles and practicing different tasks

818
00:42:41,010 --> 00:42:43,730
depending on what state you're in

819
00:42:43,730 --> 00:42:46,090
So for example, if you have a robot

820
00:42:46,090 --> 00:42:50,130
that's trying to make coffee, maybe the first task is to put--

821
00:42:50,130 --> 00:42:54,450
or the first subtask is to put the cup in the coffee machine

822
00:42:54,450 --> 00:42:57,530
And maybe if it attempts that, it actually

823
00:42:57,530 --> 00:43:00,410
drops the cup on accident

824
00:43:00,410 --> 00:43:02,530
And then, traditionally, what you would do

825
00:43:02,530 --> 00:43:05,330
is you would have a human reset the cup back

826
00:43:05,330 --> 00:43:07,250
to where it started

827
00:43:07,250 --> 00:43:09,720
But we don't want to do that

828
00:43:09,720 --> 00:43:12,640
And so, instead, maybe the second task

829
00:43:12,640 --> 00:43:14,740
is instead of-- because it made a mistake

830
00:43:14,740 --> 00:43:17,620
the second task shouldn't be to turn on the coffee machine

831
00:43:17,620 --> 00:43:20,750
Instead, it should be to pick up the cup

832
00:43:20,750 --> 00:43:22,920
And so we can imagine having these different cycles

833
00:43:22,920 --> 00:43:25,800
where we practice task 1

834
00:43:25,800 --> 00:43:28,480
Then if we fail, we practice task 2

835
00:43:28,480 --> 00:43:31,620
And then task 2 maybe is resetting task 1

836
00:43:31,620 --> 00:43:33,360
We maybe then go back to task 1, which

837
00:43:33,360 --> 00:43:35,840
is to put the cup in the coffee machine

838
00:43:35,840 --> 00:43:39,760
If we didn't succeed at task 2, we might keep trying it

839
00:43:39,760 --> 00:43:42,220
If we do succeed at task 1 at putting the coffee machine

840
00:43:42,220 --> 00:43:46,840
we might move to task 3, and so on, and so forth

841
00:43:46,840 --> 00:43:50,640
And so we can have this graph or cycle of different tasks

842
00:43:50,640 --> 00:43:52,680
that we're trying, at different points in time

843
00:43:52,680 --> 00:43:54,300
depending on the state we're in

844
00:43:54,300 --> 00:43:56,040
We'll practice a different task that

845
00:43:56,040 --> 00:43:58,240
is possible to practice from that state

846
00:43:58,240 --> 00:44:05,080
and exclude tasks that are not feasible from that state

847
00:44:05,080 --> 00:44:06,960
Cool

848
00:44:06,960 --> 00:44:10,150
So what this would look like is, we

849
00:44:10,150 --> 00:44:13,230
will be learning a task condition policy, so

850
00:44:13,230 --> 00:44:15,430
like a multi-task policy

851
00:44:15,430 --> 00:44:28,350
And we will be in some state, and so we'll observe the state

852
00:44:28,350 --> 00:44:29,940
that we're in

853
00:44:29,940 --> 00:44:31,690
And then based on the state that we're in

854
00:44:31,690 --> 00:44:33,070
we'll propose a task that we want

855
00:44:33,070 --> 00:44:34,790
to practice from that state

856
00:44:34,790 --> 00:44:38,630
So we'll have a step that is proposing

857
00:44:38,630 --> 00:44:44,510
a task that we can use Zi to denote the task that we're

858
00:44:44,510 --> 00:44:46,350
proposing

859
00:44:46,350 --> 00:44:50,030
And this could be the descriptor for that task

860
00:44:50,030 --> 00:44:58,350
And then we will run the policy for that task

861
00:44:58,350 --> 00:45:03,670
And so this could be a multi-task policy

862
00:45:03,670 --> 00:45:08,820
that's conditioned on Zi

863
00:45:08,820 --> 00:45:12,180
And then we could also have an update step

864
00:45:12,180 --> 00:45:13,980
where we update our policy

865
00:45:13,980 --> 00:45:16,640
If we have more on-policy algorithm

866
00:45:16,640 --> 00:45:19,480
then this would be updating it for this particular task

867
00:45:19,480 --> 00:45:21,260
If we have an off-policy algorithm

868
00:45:21,260 --> 00:45:24,060
we could update it for any of the tasks

869
00:45:24,060 --> 00:45:26,820
and then repeat this process, where

870
00:45:26,820 --> 00:45:30,520
after we've run it for that task for some number of steps

871
00:45:30,520 --> 00:45:35,060
we'll then propose a different task and practice that task

872
00:45:35,060 --> 00:45:38,100
So initialize our policy, propose a task

873
00:45:38,100 --> 00:45:41,480
roll out the policy for each steps, and then update

874
00:45:41,480 --> 00:45:43,060
And specifically, we'll be updating it

875
00:45:43,060 --> 00:45:47,900
with the reward function that takes into account the tasks

876
00:45:47,900 --> 00:45:48,970
that we're practicing

877
00:45:48,970 --> 00:45:51,140
So we'll need a reward function for all of the tasks

878
00:45:51,140 --> 00:45:54,600
that we might be doing

879
00:45:54,600 --> 00:45:55,100
Yeah

880
00:45:55,100 --> 00:45:56,950
And then, again, we won't have resets

881
00:45:56,950 --> 00:46:01,100
or we'll have a very infrequent reset

882
00:46:01,100 --> 00:46:03,100
And then, at test time, we can then

883
00:46:03,100 --> 00:46:05,660
deploy this multitask policy, which can do not just one task

884
00:46:05,660 --> 00:46:09,260
but multiple different tasks

885
00:46:09,260 --> 00:46:12,980
And as an example, this is a robot system that

886
00:46:12,980 --> 00:46:16,450
is the goal is to pick up--

887
00:46:16,450 --> 00:46:18,620
basically, it's different stages and different tasks

888
00:46:18,620 --> 00:46:19,450
or different stages

889
00:46:19,450 --> 00:46:26,420
You want to pick up this object, flip it over, and rotate it

890
00:46:26,420 --> 00:46:28,920
And there are different subtasks here

891
00:46:28,920 --> 00:46:32,160
And so at the beginning, it doesn't know how to flip it

892
00:46:32,160 --> 00:46:35,020
and it doesn't certainly doesn't know how to rotate it

893
00:46:35,020 --> 00:46:37,960
Or maybe it does actually know how to flip it

894
00:46:37,960 --> 00:46:39,840
but it isn't very good at rotating it

895
00:46:39,840 --> 00:46:41,620
It's something where, at the beginning

896
00:46:41,620 --> 00:46:43,600
you don't know how to flip it or rotate it

897
00:46:43,600 --> 00:46:46,060
and you'll basically just be like practicing picking it up

898
00:46:46,060 --> 00:46:50,580
from the hand and putting it back, picking it up, putting it

899
00:46:50,580 --> 00:46:52,220
down, put it down

900
00:46:52,220 --> 00:46:54,360
And then once you get better at picking it up

901
00:46:54,360 --> 00:46:59,100
then you'll start to actually rotate it or flip it

902
00:46:59,100 --> 00:47:00,780
over and rotate it

903
00:47:00,780 --> 00:47:03,460
And so depending on which state you're actually in you'll

904
00:47:03,460 --> 00:47:05,290
actually practice something that makes

905
00:47:05,290 --> 00:47:07,690
sense for that given state

906
00:47:07,690 --> 00:47:10,210
And this can actually address some of the challenges

907
00:47:10,210 --> 00:47:13,770
that we talked about with regard to imbalance and tasks, where

908
00:47:13,770 --> 00:47:17,170
if you haven't actually yet successfully run

909
00:47:17,170 --> 00:47:20,230
the forward policy to actually change the state

910
00:47:20,230 --> 00:47:21,650
And you may not even be in a state

911
00:47:21,650 --> 00:47:23,650
where it makes sense to run the backward policy

912
00:47:23,650 --> 00:47:27,970
then you can keep on running the policy for a task that

913
00:47:27,970 --> 00:47:30,450
makes sense

914
00:47:30,450 --> 00:47:32,210
As a couple examples-- more examples

915
00:47:32,210 --> 00:47:35,830
of this, this example on the left

916
00:47:35,830 --> 00:47:40,170
you're trying to learn both for walking forward and walking

917
00:47:40,170 --> 00:47:41,130
backwards

918
00:47:41,130 --> 00:47:46,890
And you also, at the same time, are actually

919
00:47:46,890 --> 00:47:48,690
learning how to get back up

920
00:47:48,690 --> 00:47:51,130
And so you can see it kind of transitioning between going

921
00:47:51,130 --> 00:47:52,570
forward, going backward

922
00:47:52,570 --> 00:47:54,950
If it's too far to the back, then it

923
00:47:54,950 --> 00:47:56,830
will doesn't want to keep on going backwards

924
00:47:56,830 --> 00:48:00,370
and so then you'll practice the task of going forwards

925
00:48:00,370 --> 00:48:03,420
And likewise, this example on the right, there's actually

926
00:48:03,420 --> 00:48:04,880
it's like a seven-stage test, where

927
00:48:04,880 --> 00:48:11,640
you need to pick it up, position it, and screw repeatedly

928
00:48:11,640 --> 00:48:13,680
And so it's running these different policies

929
00:48:13,680 --> 00:48:18,520
or these different controllers based on the state that it's in

930
00:48:18,520 --> 00:48:22,320
And eventually, it will also run the backward policy of putting

931
00:48:22,320 --> 00:48:25,500
the wrench back on the table

932
00:48:25,500 --> 00:48:26,000
Yeah

933
00:48:26,000 --> 00:48:30,560
So the question is, how do we select these tasks, basically

934
00:48:30,560 --> 00:48:35,740
Yeah, basically, there are two approaches

935
00:48:35,740 --> 00:48:36,780
that have been done here

936
00:48:36,780 --> 00:48:41,720
And this is also at the bleeding edge of research in some ways

937
00:48:41,720 --> 00:48:44,120
The verses that you could predefine a set of tasks

938
00:48:44,120 --> 00:48:47,900
and also predefine that graph, saying if you failed task 1

939
00:48:47,900 --> 00:48:49,880
then you should go should practice task 2

940
00:48:49,880 --> 00:48:52,320
And basically, upfront, predefine

941
00:48:52,320 --> 00:48:56,760
that graph of what you should do in different circumstances

942
00:48:56,760 --> 00:48:59,520
and then act according to that graph

943
00:48:59,520 --> 00:49:03,070
Alternatively, you could also train a model that

944
00:49:03,070 --> 00:49:05,430
proposes tasks for you to do

945
00:49:05,430 --> 00:49:08,470
And so, for example, in general, I

946
00:49:08,470 --> 00:49:11,030
think it's an open question, with regard to which tasks

947
00:49:11,030 --> 00:49:13,870
you pick to collect for at a given point in time

948
00:49:13,870 --> 00:49:16,280
But you could, for example, ask a language model to say

949
00:49:16,280 --> 00:49:18,030
what are the different things that I could

950
00:49:18,030 --> 00:49:21,630
do in this environment to get better and actually

951
00:49:21,630 --> 00:49:25,550
then practice the tasks that it describes as output

952
00:49:25,550 --> 00:49:31,070
And so as an example of that, if we are at--

953
00:49:31,070 --> 00:49:33,560
if this is the frame that we're in

954
00:49:33,560 --> 00:49:35,890
then there's certain things that we can do in this task

955
00:49:35,890 --> 00:49:38,290
We could open this toy microwave

956
00:49:38,290 --> 00:49:39,490
We could pick up the banana

957
00:49:39,490 --> 00:49:42,150
We could push the eggplant, move the pot

958
00:49:42,150 --> 00:49:44,710
But if we instead told it to pick up the carrot

959
00:49:44,710 --> 00:49:47,470
that's not something that you can do in the scene

960
00:49:47,470 --> 00:49:49,070
And if we're trying to actually train

961
00:49:49,070 --> 00:49:50,490
it to do lots of different things

962
00:49:50,490 --> 00:49:52,950
these tasks are going to be seen dependent

963
00:49:52,950 --> 00:49:57,430
So then you could ask a VLM to understand--

964
00:49:57,430 --> 00:49:59,190
think about what are the things that you

965
00:49:59,190 --> 00:50:02,250
can do in this environment, and then have it predict

966
00:50:02,250 --> 00:50:06,110
for example, take the banana out of the silver pot

967
00:50:06,110 --> 00:50:07,870
And then from there, actually, have

968
00:50:07,870 --> 00:50:11,790
it practice doing this task versus practicing

969
00:50:11,790 --> 00:50:13,530
doing some other task

970
00:50:13,530 --> 00:50:15,110
The question is, how is this different

971
00:50:15,110 --> 00:50:16,550
from hierarchical learning

972
00:50:16,550 --> 00:50:19,930
So it's a good question

973
00:50:19,930 --> 00:50:23,210
So this task proposer, you can think of it--

974
00:50:23,210 --> 00:50:25,750
it shares some similarities with the high-level policy that's

975
00:50:25,750 --> 00:50:28,670
telling you what to do in a certain circumstance

976
00:50:28,670 --> 00:50:30,570
But its purpose is actually quite different

977
00:50:30,570 --> 00:50:34,390
So the purpose here is actually having it propose things

978
00:50:34,390 --> 00:50:36,230
that you could do in the environment

979
00:50:36,230 --> 00:50:37,910
that you could practice

980
00:50:37,910 --> 00:50:40,510
Whereas, in a hierarchical system

981
00:50:40,510 --> 00:50:42,470
the high-level policy goal is to say like

982
00:50:42,470 --> 00:50:45,630
what is the next subtask that will achieve some outcome

983
00:50:45,630 --> 00:50:46,660
at the end of the day

984
00:50:46,660 --> 00:50:48,030
And so this is actually something

985
00:50:48,030 --> 00:50:50,830
where you want it to give you lots of different things

986
00:50:50,830 --> 00:50:53,350
that allow you to learn lots of different things

987
00:50:53,350 --> 00:50:55,270
Whereas, a high-level policy, you're much more

988
00:50:55,270 --> 00:50:56,890
goal directed in terms of accomplishing

989
00:50:56,890 --> 00:50:59,470
some long-term objective

990
00:50:59,470 --> 00:51:03,980
This particular work chooses to actually use

991
00:51:03,980 --> 00:51:06,900
a hierarchical method

992
00:51:06,900 --> 00:51:12,100
It's actually builds upon one of the works

993
00:51:12,100 --> 00:51:14,740
that we talked about previously, where it actually then feeds

994
00:51:14,740 --> 00:51:18,060
this into an image editing diffusion model

995
00:51:18,060 --> 00:51:20,380
to generate an image of a generated goal

996
00:51:20,380 --> 00:51:25,790
And then it trains the policy to try to reach this goal image

997
00:51:25,790 --> 00:51:27,620
And one of the things that's nice about this

998
00:51:27,620 --> 00:51:30,820
is that with this approach, you need

999
00:51:30,820 --> 00:51:35,660
to update this, using a reward function for each task

1000
00:51:35,660 --> 00:51:39,100
And it can be quite challenging to define a reward function

1001
00:51:39,100 --> 00:51:41,100
for all of the different tasks

1002
00:51:41,100 --> 00:51:43,460
Whereas, if you have an image of the task

1003
00:51:43,460 --> 00:51:44,840
that you're trying to accomplish

1004
00:51:44,840 --> 00:51:47,540
then your reward function can be based on whether or not

1005
00:51:47,540 --> 00:51:49,820
you're close to this image

1006
00:51:49,820 --> 00:51:52,860
And so it makes it easier to define the reward functions

1007
00:51:52,860 --> 00:51:59,050
Your question is, would this proposer give those outputs

1008
00:51:59,050 --> 00:51:59,810
Yeah

1009
00:51:59,810 --> 00:52:00,010
Yeah

1010
00:52:00,010 --> 00:52:00,640
So, absolutely

1011
00:52:00,640 --> 00:52:05,150
So this framework was applied to these simple robot scenes

1012
00:52:05,150 --> 00:52:07,800
But you could also, for example, like in the hockey example

1013
00:52:07,800 --> 00:52:09,170
ask it, like, what are the things

1014
00:52:09,170 --> 00:52:10,430
I could do in this situation

1015
00:52:10,430 --> 00:52:11,990
Should I practice passing

1016
00:52:11,990 --> 00:52:14,010
Should I practice shooting

1017
00:52:14,010 --> 00:52:15,850
For those particular examples, I think

1018
00:52:15,850 --> 00:52:19,470
it's also reasonable to just-- there's only

1019
00:52:19,470 --> 00:52:21,590
limited number of things that you need to practice

1020
00:52:21,590 --> 00:52:24,050
and so you could just have one session on practicing goals

1021
00:52:24,050 --> 00:52:26,490
one session on passing

1022
00:52:26,490 --> 00:52:28,450
But, yeah, you could also use an idea like this

1023
00:52:28,450 --> 00:52:34,180
to actually propose what to try to do in different situations

1024
00:52:34,180 --> 00:52:36,390
Those are a couple of autonomous learning algorithms

1025
00:52:36,390 --> 00:52:37,990
the first category being algorithms

1026
00:52:37,990 --> 00:52:39,570
where you try to do the task, and then

1027
00:52:39,570 --> 00:52:41,620
undo the task in various ways

1028
00:52:41,620 --> 00:52:43,410
The second, where you're trying to then try

1029
00:52:43,410 --> 00:52:46,050
to do one task and then another task, and then another task

1030
00:52:46,050 --> 00:52:48,260
and try to just get better at lots of different tasks

1031
00:52:48,260 --> 00:52:50,030
all at once

1032
00:52:50,030 --> 00:52:51,570
There's also actually some scenarios

1033
00:52:51,570 --> 00:52:53,530
where you can actually set up the task in a way

1034
00:52:53,530 --> 00:52:55,690
that you can do it repeatedly

1035
00:52:55,690 --> 00:53:00,410
And there isn't necessarily a lot

1036
00:53:00,410 --> 00:53:02,540
of research on this particular idea

1037
00:53:02,540 --> 00:53:04,210
but in this particular example, the goal

1038
00:53:04,210 --> 00:53:09,210
is to fold napkins for businesses, where you

1039
00:53:09,210 --> 00:53:11,570
need to fold lots of napkins

1040
00:53:11,570 --> 00:53:14,410
So those are all circumstances, where we want to learn a policy

1041
00:53:14,410 --> 00:53:18,610
And like our goal is to get a good policy rather than simply

1042
00:53:18,610 --> 00:53:22,930
the Mars Rover example, where we want to maximize rewards

1043
00:53:22,930 --> 00:53:27,530
There's also the second category of problems

1044
00:53:27,530 --> 00:53:31,170
and the motivation is that we've been trying to learn policies

1045
00:53:31,170 --> 00:53:32,190
without resets

1046
00:53:32,190 --> 00:53:33,820
You'd improve the quality of the policy

1047
00:53:33,820 --> 00:53:35,930
through practice, but then at some point

1048
00:53:35,930 --> 00:53:38,250
we're actually going to be deploying the policy

1049
00:53:38,250 --> 00:53:41,050
And ideally, this policy will be pretty good

1050
00:53:41,050 --> 00:53:44,190
And maybe you're in a scenario where, obviously, everything's

1051
00:53:44,190 --> 00:53:45,190
going to work perfectly

1052
00:53:45,190 --> 00:53:47,650
Nothing will ever go wrong, when you actually go and deploy

1053
00:53:47,650 --> 00:53:49,130
your policy

1054
00:53:49,130 --> 00:53:51,130
But in practice, that's not the case

1055
00:53:51,130 --> 00:53:54,390
And the natural world, things will go wrong

1056
00:53:54,390 --> 00:53:57,040
despite having a lot of training

1057
00:53:57,040 --> 00:53:59,640
And this latter setting is thinking about

1058
00:53:59,640 --> 00:54:02,800
after we've learned some sort of policy

1059
00:54:02,800 --> 00:54:05,440
when it's out in the world on its own

1060
00:54:05,440 --> 00:54:11,120
can it recover from mistakes and learn after the deployment

1061
00:54:11,120 --> 00:54:13,560
process

1062
00:54:13,560 --> 00:54:17,620
And this is something that even humans do to some extent

1063
00:54:17,620 --> 00:54:21,840
So here's a video where a person is trying to open a door

1064
00:54:21,840 --> 00:54:23,420
And if you look really carefully

1065
00:54:23,420 --> 00:54:26,080
you'll notice that, actually, the person makes a mistake

1066
00:54:26,080 --> 00:54:30,000
and then adapts, really, in a split second in order

1067
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:31,720
to accomplish the task

1068
00:54:31,720 --> 00:54:33,600
So if you play this video again, we'll

1069
00:54:33,600 --> 00:54:37,240
see that right here, the person is trying to apply force

1070
00:54:37,240 --> 00:54:39,840
and the key actually isn't correctly in the hole

1071
00:54:39,840 --> 00:54:45,400
And then they try again by removing the key backwards

1072
00:54:45,400 --> 00:54:49,080
and then putting it back into the keyhole

1073
00:54:49,080 --> 00:54:52,520
And there's lots of examples like this, hopefully

1074
00:54:52,520 --> 00:54:57,830
that you've encountered in your own deployed policy

1075
00:54:57,830 --> 00:54:59,830
And we'd like to think about this setting, where

1076
00:54:59,830 --> 00:55:06,150
we could actually adapt, within an episode, during deployment

1077
00:55:06,150 --> 00:55:08,570
So in terms of how this compares to previous algorithms

1078
00:55:08,570 --> 00:55:09,630
we've talked about--

1079
00:55:09,630 --> 00:55:11,130
a lot of the course has been talking

1080
00:55:11,130 --> 00:55:13,630
about this episodic setting, where you attempt it

1081
00:55:13,630 --> 00:55:15,270
from the initial state repeatedly

1082
00:55:15,270 --> 00:55:17,190
and then deploy the policy once

1083
00:55:17,190 --> 00:55:20,670
We also talked today about this reset-free setting

1084
00:55:20,670 --> 00:55:23,970
where you learn how to attempt the task

1085
00:55:23,970 --> 00:55:29,220
undo the task repeatedly, and then want to deploy your policy

1086
00:55:29,220 --> 00:55:31,350
And this more recent setting is something where you

1087
00:55:31,350 --> 00:55:32,690
have some prior training data

1088
00:55:32,690 --> 00:55:35,910
Maybe you've trained a policy, and you're

1089
00:55:35,910 --> 00:55:39,510
in some new out-of-distribution circumstance

1090
00:55:39,510 --> 00:55:44,850
And as you're trying to actually do the task, because you're

1091
00:55:44,850 --> 00:55:47,430
in some new circumstances, you need to do some problem solving

1092
00:55:47,430 --> 00:55:51,750
and actually adapt and learn during deployment

1093
00:55:51,750 --> 00:55:57,540
during a single episode without resets

1094
00:55:57,540 --> 00:56:00,740
And so you can think of this as after previous data

1095
00:56:00,740 --> 00:56:04,260
or previous training, you have one life to autonomously

1096
00:56:04,260 --> 00:56:08,460
complete the task

1097
00:56:08,460 --> 00:56:10,780
As a fairly simple example, maybe you

1098
00:56:10,780 --> 00:56:13,660
have training experience in a flat

1099
00:56:13,660 --> 00:56:15,570
like running in like flat ground

1100
00:56:15,570 --> 00:56:17,700
And then at some point, you encounter some obstacle

1101
00:56:17,700 --> 00:56:19,740
that you hadn't seen before, and you

1102
00:56:19,740 --> 00:56:22,300
need to figure out how to get over this obstacle

1103
00:56:22,300 --> 00:56:24,430
despite not having seen this during training

1104
00:56:24,430 --> 00:56:26,180
And you want to use reinforcement learning

1105
00:56:26,180 --> 00:56:27,860
or use some ideas from reinforcement

1106
00:56:27,860 --> 00:56:30,460
learning to adapt to the new circumstances

1107
00:56:30,460 --> 00:56:35,020
live within an episode

1108
00:56:35,020 --> 00:56:39,560
So one thing we might think about is, can we just run--

1109
00:56:39,560 --> 00:56:41,980
can we continue to fine tune our policy with reinforcement

1110
00:56:41,980 --> 00:56:44,260
learning at test time

1111
00:56:44,260 --> 00:56:45,720
If, for example, we make a mistake

1112
00:56:45,720 --> 00:56:47,180
maybe we can just update our policy

1113
00:56:47,180 --> 00:56:51,860
with TD updates or policy gradients

1114
00:56:51,860 --> 00:56:56,780
live, as we're rolling out our policy

1115
00:56:56,780 --> 00:56:59,980
And so if we actually do that-- so we train a policy

1116
00:56:59,980 --> 00:57:06,860
and then try to fine tune it at test time with this obstacle

1117
00:57:06,860 --> 00:57:08,440
we get something that looks like this

1118
00:57:08,440 --> 00:57:10,420
So the policy actually does figure out

1119
00:57:10,420 --> 00:57:12,040
how to get over the first obstacle

1120
00:57:12,040 --> 00:57:16,300
even though it has never seen that obstacle before

1121
00:57:16,300 --> 00:57:18,260
And then, eventually, it actually

1122
00:57:18,260 --> 00:57:21,620
gets stuck on flipped over

1123
00:57:21,620 --> 00:57:24,380
And even though it's running reinforcement learning

1124
00:57:24,380 --> 00:57:25,540
and you can see it's--

1125
00:57:25,540 --> 00:57:28,020
it is somewhat trying to get up

1126
00:57:28,020 --> 00:57:30,460
It essentially gets stuck

1127
00:57:30,460 --> 00:57:32,540
And even though you're still running reinforcement

1128
00:57:32,540 --> 00:57:34,000
learning, still running the policy

1129
00:57:34,000 --> 00:57:36,220
and still running updates, it isn't

1130
00:57:36,220 --> 00:57:38,940
able to recover from these states

1131
00:57:38,940 --> 00:57:42,160
that it hasn't seen before, without on its own

1132
00:57:42,160 --> 00:57:43,810
without interventions

1133
00:57:43,810 --> 00:57:46,020
And ideally, we'd have a policy that could figure out

1134
00:57:46,020 --> 00:57:50,300
how to get up and how to retry during deployment

1135
00:57:50,300 --> 00:57:54,210
in these new circumstances

1136
00:57:54,210 --> 00:57:58,810
Now this is also something that's

1137
00:57:58,810 --> 00:58:00,490
bleeding edge of research

1138
00:58:00,490 --> 00:58:03,330
And I'll just mention a few ideas that have been proposed

1139
00:58:03,330 --> 00:58:09,410
that seem to be quite helpful for addressing this problem

1140
00:58:09,410 --> 00:58:14,090
One is that when you're getting stuck, you end up in this state

1141
00:58:14,090 --> 00:58:15,870
that it's hard to recover from

1142
00:58:15,870 --> 00:58:18,490
And if you're doing reinforcement learning updates

1143
00:58:18,490 --> 00:58:20,650
say that you are running your policy

1144
00:58:20,650 --> 00:58:23,410
and somehow, you get stuck right here

1145
00:58:23,410 --> 00:58:25,490
If you're running reinforcement learning updates

1146
00:58:25,490 --> 00:58:27,990
you are running reinforcement learning updates

1147
00:58:27,990 --> 00:58:31,450
with regard to the task of getting to the goal

1148
00:58:31,450 --> 00:58:35,610
And if instead of trying to run RL updates to get to the goal

1149
00:58:35,610 --> 00:58:39,690
if you instead tried to run RL updates to get back to states

1150
00:58:39,690 --> 00:58:42,650
that are familiar, like good states that you visited

1151
00:58:42,650 --> 00:58:44,570
in your previous experience, then

1152
00:58:44,570 --> 00:58:47,170
you might have a better time at getting unstuck

1153
00:58:47,170 --> 00:58:49,010
where, for example, these states might

1154
00:58:49,010 --> 00:58:52,360
be closer to the ones that are at the goal

1155
00:58:52,360 --> 00:58:55,740
and help perturb the agent back to something that it knows

1156
00:58:55,740 --> 00:58:59,360
So that it could then continue doing the task

1157
00:58:59,360 --> 00:59:01,760
So that's one idea

1158
00:59:01,760 --> 00:59:04,800
And then another idea is to try to leverage pre-training

1159
00:59:04,800 --> 00:59:09,880
So if you're trying to run reinforcement learning

1160
00:59:09,880 --> 00:59:13,520
on the policy weights, like in the low-level action space

1161
00:59:13,520 --> 00:59:15,840
you can only change the policy so much

1162
00:59:15,840 --> 00:59:19,400
And there's only so many ways you can adapt

1163
00:59:19,400 --> 00:59:21,800
And if instead, if you could adapt in a higher level

1164
00:59:21,800 --> 00:59:25,400
space, if you had some set of pre-trained skills

1165
00:59:25,400 --> 00:59:27,960
then you might be able to actually adapt more drastically

1166
00:59:27,960 --> 00:59:30,470
and actually try to change a strategy that you're taking

1167
00:59:30,470 --> 00:59:32,640
rather than just changing the weights or the actions

1168
00:59:32,640 --> 00:59:34,240
that you're using

1169
00:59:34,240 --> 00:59:38,120
So, for example, if you learned a skill for a pre-trained skill

1170
00:59:38,120 --> 00:59:40,400
for walking forward and a pre-trained skill

1171
00:59:40,400 --> 00:59:43,680
for getting back on your feet, then instead of trying

1172
00:59:43,680 --> 00:59:46,260
to continue walk forward in that new state

1173
00:59:46,260 --> 00:59:49,000
if you tried to explore the different skills that you have

1174
00:59:49,000 --> 00:59:51,230
and explore the state of getting back up

1175
00:59:51,230 --> 00:59:52,870
then you might be able to actually

1176
00:59:52,870 --> 00:59:55,720
be more successful at handling these circumstances

1177
00:59:55,720 --> 00:59:58,390
Because you're basically able to try different strategies rather

1178
00:59:58,390 --> 01:00:02,630
than just trying different actions or different weights

1179
01:00:02,630 --> 01:00:05,880
The other thing here is that pre-trained LLMs

1180
01:00:05,880 --> 01:00:07,670
might have some common sense reasoning that

1181
01:00:07,670 --> 01:00:12,210
could allow an agent to adapt a novel circumstances as well

1182
01:00:12,210 --> 01:00:14,350
and try different strategies

1183
01:00:14,350 --> 01:00:15,990
So let's look at a couple examples

1184
01:00:15,990 --> 01:00:18,230
of these ideas in practice

1185
01:00:18,230 --> 01:00:19,710
So if we take the first idea that's

1186
01:00:19,710 --> 01:00:22,190
trying to guide the agent with reinforcement learning

1187
01:00:22,190 --> 01:00:25,390
to familiar states rather than to the goal

1188
01:00:25,390 --> 01:00:29,110
we can actually run it on that same example

1189
01:00:29,110 --> 01:00:33,200
And we see that the agent does actually--

1190
01:00:33,200 --> 01:00:35,570
I mean, this is still an out-of-distribution environment

1191
01:00:35,570 --> 01:00:38,890
It does get stuck on its back, but it is able to, for example

1192
01:00:38,890 --> 01:00:43,050
get back up after trying

1193
01:00:43,050 --> 01:00:45,250
So here, it's stuck on its back, and it's

1194
01:00:45,250 --> 01:00:47,170
trying to get back to a state that's familiar

1195
01:00:47,170 --> 01:00:49,390
for example, a state where it's on its feet

1196
01:00:49,390 --> 01:00:51,270
And it's able to eventually recover

1197
01:00:51,270 --> 01:00:56,270
from that after a bit of attempts

1198
01:00:56,270 --> 01:01:02,430
and then make it to the goal state

1199
01:01:02,430 --> 01:01:04,600
And then in terms of adapting, we

1200
01:01:04,600 --> 01:01:06,310
talked about adopting the low-level space

1201
01:01:06,310 --> 01:01:08,550
and in the high-level space

1202
01:01:08,550 --> 01:01:10,230
This first example is a system that

1203
01:01:10,230 --> 01:01:14,750
was trained to be able to adapt in the low-level space

1204
01:01:14,750 --> 01:01:16,870
from recent history

1205
01:01:16,870 --> 01:01:20,870
And this is able to handle some small out-of-distribution

1206
01:01:20,870 --> 01:01:24,550
scenarios, where it's like on different terrains

1207
01:01:24,550 --> 01:01:28,030
But if you have things that are more drastically

1208
01:01:28,030 --> 01:01:29,790
different from what you've seen before

1209
01:01:29,790 --> 01:01:33,750
it can be very helpful to adapt in a more high-level space

1210
01:01:33,750 --> 01:01:36,670
And so what that could look like is say that you--

1211
01:01:36,670 --> 01:01:38,710
say that you have this legged robot that's trying

1212
01:01:38,710 --> 01:01:40,710
to reach a goal over here

1213
01:01:40,710 --> 01:01:44,990
Then, if for example, all the agencies

1214
01:01:44,990 --> 01:01:48,160
is this image right here tries to go forward

1215
01:01:48,160 --> 01:01:50,100
goes backward, if you just keep on running

1216
01:01:50,100 --> 01:01:52,300
a policy without any context, it'll just

1217
01:01:52,300 --> 01:01:53,820
keep on trying to go forward

1218
01:01:53,820 --> 01:01:59,300
Because it doesn't know that it tried going forward before

1219
01:01:59,300 --> 01:02:01,460
If instead you try different strategies

1220
01:02:01,460 --> 01:02:05,020
like moving left, moving backwards, with context

1221
01:02:05,020 --> 01:02:09,340
and adaptation, then you can figure out how to, ultimately--

1222
01:02:09,340 --> 01:02:11,260
Even though the agent in this particular case

1223
01:02:11,260 --> 01:02:14,020
has never seen this environment before, it's able to problem

1224
01:02:14,020 --> 01:02:17,780
solve its way through these different obstacles

1225
01:02:17,780 --> 01:02:21,900
and navigating to a goal position

1226
01:02:21,900 --> 01:02:24,540
This is something where if you adapt it in the low-level motor

1227
01:02:24,540 --> 01:02:30,180
space, trying out different positions of your joints, then

1228
01:02:30,180 --> 01:02:32,740
it would be very tricky to try to problem solve

1229
01:02:32,740 --> 01:02:35,400
through this circumstance

1230
01:02:35,400 --> 01:02:35,900
Cool

1231
01:02:35,900 --> 01:02:40,780
So that was a brief foray into this single-life reinforcement

1232
01:02:40,780 --> 01:02:42,260
learning setting

1233
01:02:42,260 --> 01:02:43,120
Yeah, good question

1234
01:02:43,120 --> 01:02:45,560
So how do we bake in safety constraints

1235
01:02:45,560 --> 01:02:48,610
So yeah, another circumstance that

1236
01:02:48,610 --> 01:02:53,010
might require human oversight is, if you're in scenarios

1237
01:02:53,010 --> 01:02:57,050
where you could make safety critical mistakes-- if you

1238
01:02:57,050 --> 01:03:02,250
are a legged robot, you can fall in a way that damages the robot

1239
01:03:02,250 --> 01:03:05,290
If you're a manipulator, you could drop a glass

1240
01:03:05,290 --> 01:03:06,330
onto the floor

1241
01:03:06,330 --> 01:03:09,730
You could also hit objects pretty hard

1242
01:03:09,730 --> 01:03:14,740
There's actually work that builds very directly

1243
01:03:14,740 --> 01:03:16,570
upon some of these ideas, where you actually

1244
01:03:16,570 --> 01:03:19,050
try to learn some notion of safety

1245
01:03:19,050 --> 01:03:21,470
and learn when you're actually in a high risk state

1246
01:03:21,470 --> 01:03:24,990
and then go back to a state that is less risky

1247
01:03:24,990 --> 01:03:28,010
So you could actually learn, for example, a recovery policy that

1248
01:03:28,010 --> 01:03:30,050
tries to get back to states that are known

1249
01:03:30,050 --> 01:03:33,290
to be less risky and more safe

1250
01:03:33,290 --> 01:03:36,050
And so these ideas, actually-- some of these ideas

1251
01:03:36,050 --> 01:03:44,170
were actually, several years ago, studied in safety settings

1252
01:03:44,170 --> 01:03:45,680
The other practical thing to do is

1253
01:03:45,680 --> 01:03:49,960
to try to, to the extent possible

1254
01:03:49,960 --> 01:03:52,040
try to impose safety constraints on the controller

1255
01:03:52,040 --> 01:03:54,440
itself so that, for example, you're

1256
01:03:54,440 --> 01:03:58,960
not using the motors to the point in which they'll overheat

1257
01:03:58,960 --> 01:04:02,200
or minimize, have a box around the robot that doesn't allow it

1258
01:04:02,200 --> 01:04:05,960
to, for example, apply a lot of force

1259
01:04:05,960 --> 01:04:08,200
to different parts of the state space

1260
01:04:08,200 --> 01:04:09,700
Now, that's not possible everywhere

1261
01:04:09,700 --> 01:04:14,120
And learning notions of safety might be the only way

1262
01:04:14,120 --> 01:04:16,700
to actually address the problem in general

1263
01:04:16,700 --> 01:04:20,100
Although to learn safety, you need examples of failing

1264
01:04:20,100 --> 01:04:23,880
So it's generally a pretty tricky problem

1265
01:04:23,880 --> 01:04:27,640
And yeah, I guess those are my main thoughts on that

1266
01:04:27,640 --> 01:04:30,220
Yeah, so you also-- the question is maybe

1267
01:04:30,220 --> 01:04:31,800
when navigate around people, you could

1268
01:04:31,800 --> 01:04:36,080
try to ask a VLM, like what you might

1269
01:04:36,080 --> 01:04:38,500
try to do at a high level in different circumstances

1270
01:04:38,500 --> 01:04:42,200
And this work, right here, actually

1271
01:04:42,200 --> 01:04:45,680
was using a VLM to indicate with past experience what

1272
01:04:45,680 --> 01:04:47,520
are good strategies to try

1273
01:04:47,520 --> 01:04:51,400
And so these models may have some common sense, suggesting

1274
01:04:51,400 --> 01:04:56,200
maybe you shouldn't walk or run into a person, for example

1275
01:04:56,200 --> 01:04:59,320
Or maybe you should wait, that sort of thing

1276
01:04:59,320 --> 01:05:04,320
And so leveraging those common sense in those models

1277
01:05:04,320 --> 01:05:07,160
is fairly promising

1278
01:05:07,160 --> 01:05:08,520
Cool

1279
01:05:08,520 --> 01:05:09,020
Great

1280
01:05:09,020 --> 01:05:12,680
So to summarize, we talked about why reinforcement learning

1281
01:05:12,680 --> 01:05:14,960
algorithms don't already give robots autonomous

1282
01:05:14,960 --> 01:05:16,840
learning capabilities

1283
01:05:16,840 --> 01:05:20,880
And define the problem, define a couple instances

1284
01:05:20,880 --> 01:05:22,560
of this problem, where we either want

1285
01:05:22,560 --> 01:05:25,600
to think about the lifetime of rewards or learning

1286
01:05:25,600 --> 01:05:26,780
a good policy

1287
01:05:26,780 --> 01:05:28,400
And we talked about algorithms for each

1288
01:05:28,400 --> 01:05:31,220
of those, where we try to, for example

1289
01:05:31,220 --> 01:05:34,080
learn a backward policy, learn a task graph

1290
01:05:34,080 --> 01:05:37,040
and try different tasks, and also single life

1291
01:05:37,040 --> 01:05:39,310
RL to prevent drift

